WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
The Shining has attracted an almost cult-like
collective of fans who long to discover its

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:05.800
secrets.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.160
What follows are our favorite fan theories
we've gathered up from internet forums, fan

00:00:10.171 --> 00:00:14.830
films and documentaries like Room 237, and
just putting the movie on repeat until we

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.100
start coming up with our own kooky headcanons.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.780
Out of all the theories out there about the
true meaning of The Shining, one of the more

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.690
plausible ones is that the film is an allegorical
tale about the genocide of Native Americans

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:27.960
by white settlers.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.320
The most overt hint occurs when Stuart Ullman,
a smiling white guy who dresses in red, white,

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:37.920
and blue, and has an American flag on his
desk, casually mentions that the Overlook

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.660
is literally built over dead Native Americans.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.010
"The site is supposed to be located on an
Indian burial ground, and I believe they actually

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.000
had to repel a few Indian attacks as they
were building it."

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.980
Throughout the hotel are depictions of Native
Americans and Native American art motifs,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.560
such as the enormous sand painting on the
wall of the Colorado Lounge.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.840
Jack even uses the phrase "White Man's Burden,"
in a conversation with Lloyd, a reference

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.340
to a poem by Rudyard Kipling that valorizes
the colonization of natives by white imperialists,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.800
Internet film scholar Kevin McLeod, who did
a commentary track for the home video release

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.960
of the documentary Room 237, also claims that
the film features a pair of hidden symbols

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.900
from another group of indigenous Americans,
the Maya.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.110
He asserts that in the iconic bright yellow
film poster for The Shining, the extra wide

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:30.350
letter "T" in the title is actually a Mayan
symbol, and that a repeating pattern in the

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.080
carpet of the Gold Room, a cross within a
square, is actually the symbol for "yellow."

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.740
There are also a handful of strange but compelling
parallels between the film and the real life

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.920
founding of Point Pleasant, West Virginia.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.120
This was a horrific, bloody event with countless
Native Americans casualties, but perhaps the

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.120
most famous Native person killed there was
Hokolesqua, also known as Chief Cornstalk.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:58.880
In 1777, Hokolesqua was killed by a mob seeking
revenge for an attack that he'd had no part

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:04.440
of, and, of course, legend states that Hokolesqua's
final words were a curse on the land that

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.220
would leave it, quote, "paralyzed by the stain
of our blood."

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.680
"There are two things I know about white people:
They love Rachael Ray, and they are terrified

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.000
of curses."

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.900
This statement has clear parallels to the
famous wave of blood from the film, but believe

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.280
it or not, it's not the biggest link between
the battle of Point Pleasant and The Shining.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.760
In 1909, the same year the fictional Overlook
Hotel was completed in the film, a real life

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.939
monument in Point Pleasant was struck by lightning,
and the same thing happened again on July

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.140
4, 1921.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.379
As any true Shining fan knows, July 4, 1921,
is the date on the old photograph of Jack

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.000
from the final shot of the film.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.460
Before we go any further, let's get one thing
straight.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.020
The moon landing wasn't faked.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.440
Mythbusters did a whole episode about it..

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.880
Go watch Hidden Figures.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.180
Educate yourself.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.800
However, there has been a conspiracy theory
circulating since before The Shining came

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:03.549
out that the Apollo 11 moon landing was faked,
and that Stanley Kubrick was the one hired

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:04.780
by the government to shoot it.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.540
It would obviously be nearly impossible to
carry a secret like that without letting it

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.400
out somewhere, so could The Shining actually
be Kubrick's covert confession?

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.480
"I don't think that's true."

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.760
Well, no.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.420
but let's run down the conspiracy's favorite
evidence.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.320
There's Tang in the Overlook pantry, a product
popularized through its association with the

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.200
space program.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.889
In one scene, Danny is wearing a handmade
sweater famously depicting Apollo 11 and the

00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:32.080
Saturn V rocket.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.680
Also, during the adaptation from the book
to the film, the most haunted room in the

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:43.019
hotel changed room numbers, from 217 to 237,
and according to Shining conspiracy theorists,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.560
the distance to the moon is about 237,000
miles away.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:53.080
In truth, it's more like 238,900 miles away
on average, but we didn't know this until

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.160
the late '60s.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.640
Maybe Kubrick was looking at an old textbook
with out of date information when he and NASA

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.400
were trying to fool the entire world by faking
the most documented news story of all time.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.260
This could all be intentional, though.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:10.059
By 1980, Kubrick was most likely aware of
the conspiracy theory, since it sprung up

00:04:10.059 --> 00:04:15.840
after he directed 2001: A Space Odyssey in
1968, and he also knew how closely people

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.200
analyzed his films for hidden meanings.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.900
Maybe he decided to just mess with them a
little for fun.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.840
We all know what happens when it's all work
and no play.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.220
One of the most subtly haunting elements of
the Overlook Hotel is that its layout constantly

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.380
seems to be shifting.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.420
The bright exterior window in Ullman's office
is completely nonsensical, because his office

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:36.940
is in the middle of the building.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.540
And during a conversation between Wendy and
Jack in the Colorado Lounge, a chair in the

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.040
background keeps appearing and disappearing
again between shots.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:49.940
And when Hallorann is giving a tour to Wendy
and Danny, they step into one giant freezer,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.100
but then exit from a different freezer across
the room.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.540
Kubrick was a notoriously meticulous filmmaker,
so it seems unlikely that these strange occurrences

00:04:58.540 --> 00:04:59.640
are just mistakes.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.220
What else might they signify?

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.000
Maybe the Overlook isn't actually the Overlook
at all.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.260
Instead, it's Hell, where Jack must continually
repeat the last winter of his life forever.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.940
It's not that big of a stretch, as we already
know the hotel has at least one other imprisoned,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.400
killer ghost in the form of Grady, who sometimes
doesn't seem to remember killing his family.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.280
With this reading, many of the film's more
puzzling lines suddenly become meaningful,

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.100
like Jack having an odd familiarity with the
Overlook before he even arrives.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:35.260
"When I came up here for my interview, it
was as though I'd been here before."

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.960
Later, Grady tells him that he's always been
the caretaker, and maybe that eternity at

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.680
the hotel is his reward for the sins of his
life.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.360
Remember, unlike Wendy and Danny, Jack is
only ever seen in the Overlook or on the road

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.500
that leads up to it.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.920
Whenever you're watching The Shining, Jack
is always the caretaker.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.920
There are a lot of continuity errors and geographical
oddities in The Shining, but maybe that's

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.480
because Jack and his family are hallucinating
throughout their stay at the Overlook.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.700
In fact, maybe they're the subject of a government-run
science experiment.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.060
According to this theory, Jack's employment
at the Overlook is a front so that the US

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:16.220
government can test out mind control techniques
on isolated test subjects, and all the supernatural

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:20.660
visions the Torrances experience are actually
the results of these experiments.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.460
It's a fun theory, but there's not much evidence
to back it up, apart from a single incongruous

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:26.680
poster in the game room.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.380
It's an image of a skier above the word "Monarch,"
which is the first time Danny sees the Grady

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.560
girls in the hotel.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.301
However, during Jack's interview, Ullman claims
that there's no skiing in the area, so why

00:06:37.301 --> 00:06:39.180
would they advertise it in the game room?

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.440
And what is "Monarch?"

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.060
Well, it's actually Monarch Mountain, a ski
resort that's been open in Colorado since

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:51.450
1939, but according to conspiracy theorists,
Monarch was the name of a real-world CIA project

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.100
that attempted to discover the secrets of
mind control.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.540
In this reading of the film, the character
Bill Watson, who seems like Stuart Ullman's

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:03.000
assistant, is actually the brains of the operation,
a high-ranking government agent.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.740
Notice that Watson is called into Ullman's
office when Jack arrives, and yet he doesn't

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.880
get a job title, and he doesn't ask Jack any
questions.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:15.720
In fact, he doesn't have any dialogue at all
other than a blunt "hi" later on.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:20.320
He just sits there, watching as Ullman tells
Jack about Grady and the Overlook's creepy

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:22.900
history, planting those ideas in Jack's head.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:27.900
Later, when Ullman tells Watson to transport
some luggage, Watson almost seems annoyed,

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.680
like he's not used to taking orders.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:34.580
And when Ullman gives Jack a tour, Watson
trails behind, watching everything and saying

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:35.080
nothing.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:40.720
One unusual interpretation of The Shining
is that it's less of an adaptation of Stephen

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:45.720
King's novel and actually a modern day retelling
of the ancient Greek myth of Theseus and the

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.240
Minotaur.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.920
Just like the labyrinth that houses the Minotaur
in the myth, the Overlook has its hedge maze

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.660
where the movie hits its climax.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:58.280
As Jack chases him, Danny escapes the maze
by retracing his steps, just as Theseus escapes

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.380
the labyrinth by following back a ball of
twine that he'd been unwinding as he walked.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.000
Could this be a second meaning to the sign
outside the Gold Room that advertises "the

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:07.500
Unwinding Hours"?

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.740
At several points in the film, Jack stares
into the distance with a lowered head, but

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:16.651
with eyes pointed up, in the classic "Kubrick
stare," a pose that's very evocative of a

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.600
bull getting ready to charge.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:23.320
Some theorists even cite the "Monarch" poster
in the game room, which again is for a real-life

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:28.280
ski resort you can go to, because the skier
is a silhouette with strange proportions.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:33.060
It's a stretch, but the mythical Minotaur
was the descendant of a royal bloodline, which

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:35.700
leads to another oddly convincing connection.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.610
In the original story, the Minotaur was the
product of a union between a human and a beast,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:45.100
and, well, the audience and Wendy definitely
see something along those disturbing lines.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:48.080
Something is up with this movie and mirrors.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.840
Danny's first vision occurs in front of a
mirror.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.120
Jack recognizes the woman he's kissing in
Room 237 is a corpse by looking into a mirror.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.840
There's a long scene of Jack talking to Wendy
that takes place entirely in a reflection.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:04.960
Danny's creepy "redrum" graffiti can only
be read correctly when mirrored.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.620
The Grady girls are mirror images of each
other.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:12.520
The film even opens with a shot of a reflective
lake, mirroring the scenery above it.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.700
Perhaps the Overlook itself is a mirror, in
that it's only torturing guests with the evil

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.160
that they bring with them.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.900
That would explain why Jack, an alcoholic
with a history of abusing his son, was corrupted

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.600
by the hotel, while Danny and Wendy were not.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:30.920
Whether it's the true meaning of the movie
or not, the use of mirrors is definitely an

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:34.240
intentional illustration of one of The Shining's
deeper themes.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:38.760
The idea of finding truth through looking
backwards through history or looking into

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:43.410
mirrors occurs all throughout the movie, from
the reveal of "redrum" as a terrifying warning

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.260
to Danny escaping his father in the hedge
maze by retracing his steps.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:51.520
By looking backwards, Danny avoids becoming
a victim like the Grady children.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.340
This idea of finding truth by turning backwards
has led to some fans to try playing two projections

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:01.810
of The Shining on the same screen at the same
time, one playing forwards and one playing

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.560
backwards to see if this finally reveals the
movie's many secrets.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:11.160
It might not have led to any deeper truths,
but it has created some awesomely creepy imagery.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.180
Then again, maybe this movie is the real mirror,
and these fan theories say more about us than

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:17.240
they do about the film.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:23.020
Not all Shining theories are deadly serious
or profoundly deep, and some of them are so

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:26.180
wild that they're worth talking about for
just how weird they are.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:31.220
For example, writer and journalist Mary Katharine
Ham has a theory that the story of the Shining

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.520
has been retold more recently…as Disney's
Frozen.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:39.880
Before the story begins, our tortured protagonist
has a dark chapter in their past that looms

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:44.520
over their present, where they inadvertently
injured an innocent young family member.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:49.620
Then, that volatile character is put in charge
of running a big castle in the snowy mountains.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:54.280
Meanwhile, their innocent young family member
is locked out of a forbidden room, and passes

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:59.200
the time by playing with toys in the hallway,
and when things go bad, a wide-eyed, cheerful

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:02.100
character with prominent teeth tries to protect
them.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:07.100
Eventually, the pressure of this new job causes
our dark and tortured protagonist to snap

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.520
and turn against their family.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:13.190
The family is in over their heads, so it's
up to an experienced expert at navigating

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:17.180
the snowy wastes to climb aboard their faithful
mount to come to their rescue.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.380
In the end, both movies end with a character
getting frozen.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:25.160
In one case, it's temporary, and in another,
it's permanent, but you have to admit that

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.500
while everything might not line up, there's
enough there to have some goofy fun with.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:35.900
It's a weird, ridiculous, and truly wild "theory,"
but for some reason we just can't…let it

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:36.400
go.

